[{"id":"30171","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"792","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following is an example of homogeneous mixture?</span></p>","option_1":"Oil and water","option_2":"Salt dissolved in water","option_3":"Salt and sulphur","option_4":"Sodium chloride and iron fillings","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Salt dissolved in water.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>A homogeneous mixture is one in which the components are uniformly distributed throughout, forming a single visible phase — such as salt dissolved in water, which forms a uniform solution. Oil and water, salt and sulphur, and sodium chloride with iron filings are all heterogeneous mixtures, as their components remain visibly distinct and separable.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30172","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"792","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following bank was NOT nationalised in 1980?</span></p>","option_1":"New Bank of India Limited","option_2":"Oriental Bank of Commerce Limited","option_3":"Punjab and Sind Bank Limited","option_4":"Syndicate Bank","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Syndicate Bank.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In 1980, the Government of India nationalised six more banks (in addition to the 14 nationalised in 1969), including New Bank of India, Oriental Bank of Commerce, and Punjab and Sind Bank. Syndicate Bank, however, was nationalised earlier, in 1969, along with the first batch of 14 major banks, and hence was NOT among the banks nationalised in 1980.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30173","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"792","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following organ can be transplanted in humans?</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>I. Bone marrow</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>II. Cornea</span></p>","option_1":"Only I","option_2":"Only II","option_3":"Both I and II","option_4":"Neither I nor II","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Both I and II.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Bone marrow transplant is a well-established medical procedure used to treat various blood disorders and cancers by replacing damaged or diseased bone marrow with healthy stem cells. Cornea transplant (keratoplasty) is also a common and successful surgical procedure used to restore vision by replacing damaged corneal tissue with healthy donor tissue. Hence, both organs can be transplanted in humans.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30174","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"792","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In early Hindu philosophy, which concept governed the cycle of life from birth to death to rebirth?</span></p>","option_1":"Karma","option_2":"Yajna","option_3":"Artha","option_4":"Dharma","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Karma.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In early Hindu philosophy, the concept of \"Karma\" governed the cycle of life from birth to death to rebirth (samsara). It refers to the law of cause and effect, where a person's actions in one life determine their circumstances in future lives, forming the basis of the cycle of reincarnation.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30175","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"792","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'Velakali' is a martial dance performed in which of the following Indian state?</span></p>","option_1":"Bihar","option_2":"Kerala","option_3":"Punjab","option_4":"Mizoram","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Kerala.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'Velakali' is a traditional martial dance form performed in Kerala, characterized by vigorous movements depicting scenes from the Mahabharata, particularly associated with temple festivals in the Ambalapuzha region.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30176","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"792","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'Phagun Madai' festival is celebrated on the eve of which major festival?</span></p>","option_1":"Makar Sankranti","option_2":"Holi","option_3":"Diwali","option_4":"Lohri","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Holi.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'Phagun Madai' is a traditional festival celebrated in tribal regions (notably Bastar, Chhattisgarh) on the eve of Holi, marking the beginning of the spring festival season with traditional dance, music, and rituals specific to local tribal customs.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30177","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"792","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Why do objects fall towards the Earth?</span></p>","option_1":"Due to the Earth's rotation","option_2":"Due to the Earth's magnetic field","option_3":"Due to the force of gravity","option_4":"Due to air pressure","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Due to the force of gravity.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Objects fall towards the Earth due to the force of gravity, which is the attractive force exerted by the Earth on all objects with mass, pulling them towards its centre. This gravitational force gives objects weight and causes them to accelerate downwards when unsupported.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30178","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"792","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>After which of the following wars was the Peshwa removed and sent to Bithur?</span></p>","option_1":"First Anglo-Maratha War","option_2":"Second Anglo-Maratha War","option_3":"Third Anglo-Maratha War","option_4":"Fourth Anglo-Maratha War","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Third Anglo-Maratha War.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>After the Third Anglo-Maratha War (1817-1818), the British East India Company defeated Peshwa Baji Rao II, ending the Maratha Empire's independent existence. The Peshwa was removed from power, his territories were annexed, and he was pensioned off and sent to live in exile at Bithur, near Kanpur.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30179","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"792","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Indian Institute of Space Science and Technology was founded in which of the following year?</span></p>","option_1":"1972","option_2":"1995","option_3":"2007","option_4":"2014","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - 2007.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Indian Institute of Space Science and Technology (IIST), India's first space university, was founded in 2007 under the Department of Space, Government of India, located in Thiruvananthapuram, Kerala, to train specialized personnel for the Indian space programme.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30180","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"792","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In the Socio Economic and Caste Census (SECC) 2011, how many criteria were there for automatic inclusion of rural households?</span></p>","option_1":"5","option_2":"7","option_3":"11","option_4":"12","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - 5.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Under the Socio Economic and Caste Census (SECC) 2011, five criteria were specified for automatic inclusion of rural households into the category of priority/deprived households, such as households without a house (living in a single room with kucha walls and kucha roof), destitute/living on alms, manual scavenger households, primitive tribal groups, and legally released bonded labourers.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30181","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"792","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In which natural setting can the Tyndall effect be observed?</span></p>","option_1":"Sunlight passing through a window","option_2":"Sunlight passing through the canopy of a dense forest","option_3":"A rainbow after a rainstorm","option_4":"Light reflected from a mirror","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Sunlight passing through the canopy of a dense forest.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Tyndall effect refers to the scattering of light by particles suspended in a colloidal medium, making the path of light visible. This effect can be observed when sunlight passes through the canopy of a dense forest, where fine dust particles, water droplets, and mist in the air scatter the light, creating visible beams — a classic real-life demonstration of the Tyndall effect.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30182","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"792","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In November 2024, IL-35-mediated immunotherapy has been developed as a new treatment for which type of diabetes?</span></p>","option_1":"Type I","option_2":"Type III","option_3":"Type V","option_4":"Type VII","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Type I.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In November 2024, researchers developed IL-35-mediated immunotherapy as a new treatment approach for Type I diabetes, an autoimmune condition in which the body's immune system attacks insulin-producing cells in the pancreas. IL-35 is a regulatory cytokine being explored for its potential to modulate the immune response and protect these cells.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30183","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"792","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following port is known as the \"Queen of the Arabian Sea\"?</span></p>","option_1":"Cochin port","option_2":"Paradip","option_3":"Visakhapatnam port","option_4":"Tuticorin port","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Cochin port.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Cochin Port, located in Kerala, is popularly known as the \"Queen of the Arabian Sea\" due to its strategic location, natural harbour, and historical importance as a major trading port on the west coast of India.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30184","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"792","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>As per Indian Minerals Yearbook 2023, the largest consumer of zinc is the _________ Industry.</span></p>","option_1":"Galvanising","option_2":"Textile","option_3":"Pharmaceutical","option_4":"Plastic","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Galvanising.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>As per the Indian Minerals Yearbook 2023, the galvanising industry is the largest consumer of zinc in India, using it to coat steel and iron products to prevent corrosion — a process critical for infrastructure, automotive, and construction industries.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30185","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"792","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Thomas Cup, a Badminton Championship, was first held in _____.</span></p>","option_1":"1966","option_2":"1944","option_3":"1948","option_4":"1941","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - 1948.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Thomas Cup, the premier international men's team badminton championship, was first held in 1948-49, organized by the Badminton World Federation (then International Badminton Federation)